; resettest.eval — does `reset` really equal a new game?
;
; Run:  Dungeon.exe -eval tools\EvalScripts\resettest.eval
;
; THIS IS THE CHECK THE WHOLE RECYCLING IDEA RESTS ON. `reset` exists so a long
; run can skip the ~12-second level load between tests, and it is defined as
; "put the world where `newgame` would". That definition was chosen BECAUSE it
; can be tested: take a baseline after a real new game, wreck the world every way
; the harness knows how, reset, and take the baseline again. The two blocks must
; match line for line.
;
; WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS. An incomplete reset does not crash and does
; not print anything wrong — it quietly contaminates every later test in the run,
; and their numbers stay entirely plausible. This harness has already been bitten
; by exactly that shape once: the m_partyWiped latch survived a heal, and twelve
; ladder rungs reported 45 simulated seconds of nothing at all.
;
; READ IT BY DIFFING the two `=== BASELINE` blocks. Anything that differs is
; something `reset` failed to put back. (The RNG seed is deliberately NOT reset —
; it is how a run is DRIVEN, not what the world contains — so the script reseeds
; either side, exactly as a real suite would.)
;
; `Eval.ps1 -SelfTest` runs it twice: alone, and BATCHED after
; selftest-leavelevel.eval, which leaves the party on another level entirely (an
; ambush's "~encounter", off the world map). Its first `reset` then has a level
; to come BACK from, and both of its blocks must match the solo run's line for
; line (code-review C300: the reset used to re-read whatever level it found).
;
; THE WRECKING OPENS WITH THREE REPROS (code-review batch 77, C292 / C293), each
; a `--- repro:` echo and a `transients` the self-test reads. A real new game, a
; LOAD and a STAIR each cleared their own hand-copied list of what the level had
; under way, and only `reset` cleared all of it - so these go through the other
; three ways, the ones the A/B blocks below cannot see:
;   1. a poison gas still hanging, a sconce smashed on 5,1 and a brazier hurt and
;      burning on 9,4, then `goto crypt1` (a stair's level load): the gas must
;      not come along, and crypt1's own sconce (5,1 north) and brazier (9,4) must
;      not arrive in the wreck, the hurt or the burn - the fixture table's re-seed
;      carries an entry over BY CELL;
;   2. a gas and a bleeding monster, then a `load` of a save taken before both;
;   3. a door, a sconce, a barrel and a crate wrecked after a save, then the
;      `load`: all of them must come back whole, unhurt and not burning.
; Each has its control line first (the gas, the wreck, the effect really were
; there), so a repro that staged nothing cannot pass. And a clear line alone
; cannot tell a mended piece from a MISSING one (an empty fixture table reads
; nothing broken), so the stair and repro 3 smash what should be there
; afterwards and must count it, and repro 2 must find the wreck its save holds.
; The save is named here `resettest`; Eval.ps1 runs a copy that names it for
; this worktree, since the save folder is shared by every checkout.
;
; THEN THREE MORE (code-review batch 78, C294 / C295 / C297): what the GAME had
; running, which a new game and a load never ended and only `reset` did, by
; writing the flag. Each is the same staging - lockstep OFF, Brand hurt (so a
; rest has something to gain and cannot end itself), an undo step (the floor
; on 10,12 painted twice, so at least one paint is an edit), the kindle clock
; stepped off its phase (0.15 s), a throw's wait and a rest - then one way out:
;   4. a `load` of a save taken with Brand hurt: a rest carried over reads on;
;   5. a `newgame` (the party comes back whole, so a rest carried over stops on
;      the next frame and writes "recovered" - `rest_ended=` shows it);
;   6. a `reset` mid-rest: it used to drop the flag without handing lockstep
;      back, which only shows with lockstep OFF before the rest.
; After each: no rest, lockstep OFF again (what the rest found), no reason
; recorded, no undo or redo step, no throw's wait and the kindle clock where
; baseline A has it. The control before shows all five staged.

logecho on
; FROZEN BEFORE THE LOAD, not after it. Block B's reset happens at timescale 0
; (it carries over, being how the run is driven), so no simulated time passes
; between that reset and its readouts; with the freeze after this first reset,
; block A had one frame of it. That was invisible until `monsters` printed a
; rising skeleton's root stray (the root-motion lock), which one frame moves:
; root 0.019 against 0.002, and a "reset" that differed from nothing at all.
timescale 0
reset
lockstep on

; --- the baseline, after a real new game -------------------------------------
seed 7
; The FIRST `reset` above was a real load — nothing was loaded to recycle — so
; this baseline is a genuine new game, and the one at the bottom is a recycle.
; That is the comparison, and it is the same one either way round.
echo === BASELINE A (after a real load) ===
state
; THE LOG THE PARTY ARRIVES TO (code-review C364): a new game opens with its
; lines whether it loaded its level or found it in hand, and the recycle below
; begins play the same way (Game::BeginPlay). Without this readout the blocks
; could agree on everything but the first thing a player reads.
messages 10
party
supplies
regen
monsters
char 0
; THE MAP, and it is here because leaving it out is how the first version of
; this test passed on a world that was still an empty carved box. `arena` walls
; every cell and strips the map's fixtures, stairs and decorations; none of the
; probes above would show any of that. `mapinfo` prints the counts, and a
; walkability sample proves the CELLS came back and not just the records.
mapinfo
; WHAT A RESET MUST CLEAR AND NO OTHER READOUT SHOWS (code-review batch 12): a
; blast still spreading, a smashed door, a fall mid-plunge, the cursor's item,
; the undo history, other levels' stashes, rest, the throw and kindle clocks -
; and the level it was taken on, which is how the batched run below proves it
; came back to the harness level. Batches 77-79 inject each leak in the
; wrecking below.
transients

; --- now wreck it, every way the harness can ---------------------------------
echo === wrecking the world ===
freeze on

; --- repro 1: down a stair with a gas hanging and the fixtures wrecked -------
; crypt1 hangs a sconce on 5,1's north wall and stands a brazier on 9,4; these
; are the same kinds on the same squares of the harness level, the sconce
; smashed and the brazier hurt and burning (the re-seed carries hp and effects
; over by cell as well as the wreck).
editor place fixtures sconce 5 1 north
editor place fixtures brazier 9 4
mappage close
smash 5 1 50
smash 9 4 10
breakables 9 4 burn 0.5 30
face north
throw poison_flask
step 2
echo --- repro: before the stair ---
transients
goto crypt1
echo --- repro: down the stair ---
transients
; ...and crypt1's own sconce and brazier ARE there, whole. An EMPTY fixture
; table reads as clean as a fresh one (the false positive damage-system.md
; names), so each is smashed now: a smash that finds nothing breakable REFUSES,
; which fails the script, and the readout after must count both broken.
smash 5 1 50
smash 9 4 100
echo --- repro: crypt1's own, smashed ---
transients
goto eval_arena

; --- repro 2: a load over a gas and a bleeding monster ----------------------
; The save first, so neither is in it - but the sconce smashed and the brazier
; burning above ARE (the stair back brought them), so the load must put those
; back: a load over the same level seeds the fixture table itself, and an
; unseeded table would drop both and read as clean. skel_swarm stands authored
; on 13,13.
save resettest
face north
throw poison_flask
step 2
tp 13 12
face south
effect bleed ahead 2 30
echo --- repro: before the load ---
transients
load resettest
echo --- repro: loaded over the gas ---
transients

; --- repro 3: a door, a sconce and two props wrecked after the save ----------
; The save first, with all four whole. Then the door, the sconce and the barrel
; are smashed and the crate hurt and set alight; the load must put every one
; back as the save holds it - whole, unhurt, nothing riding it.
arena corridor 11
tp 9 12
editor place doors wooden_door 15 12
editor place fixtures sconce 17 12 north
editor place decorations barrel 12 12
editor place decorations crate 11 12
mappage close
save resettest
smash 15 12 500
smash 17 12 50
smash 12 12 50
smash 11 12 3
breakables 11 12 burn 0.5 30
echo --- repro: smashed after the save ---
transients
load resettest
echo --- repro: loaded over the smash ---
transients
; ...and every one is still THERE: a piece gone after the load reads as whole
; as a mended one, so each is smashed again (a refusal fails the script) and the
; readout after must count them all broken.
smash 15 12 500
smash 17 12 50
smash 12 12 50
smash 11 12 50
echo --- repro: smashed again after the load ---
transients

; --- repro 4: a load mid-rest, with an undo step and both clocks running ----
; The save with Brand HURT, so the loaded game has something to rest for too: a
; rest the load carried over would still read on, not stop itself.
lockstep off
setpool 0 health 10
save resettest
editor place floors floor_slabs 10 12
editor place floors floor_temple 10 12
mappage close
step 0.15
throw rock
rest on
echo --- repro: resting before the load ---
transients
load resettest
echo --- repro: loaded mid-rest ---
transients

; --- repro 5: the same, then a new game --------------------------------------
; Lockstep off again, so each repro stands on its own: after a load that kept
; the rest it would still be forced on.
lockstep off
setpool 0 health 10
editor place floors floor_slabs 10 12
editor place floors floor_temple 10 12
mappage close
step 0.15
throw rock
rest on
echo --- repro: resting before the new game ---
transients
newgame
echo --- repro: a new game mid-rest ---
transients

; --- repro 6: the same, then a reset ----------------------------------------
; Its readout straight after the reset - the echo goes first, since its marker
; is what the self-test reads from. A reset forgets `freeze` (a harness mode),
; and the wrecking below sets it again.
lockstep off
setpool 0 health 10
editor place floors floor_slabs 10 12
editor place floors floor_temple 10 12
mappage close
step 0.15
throw rock
rest on
echo --- repro: resting before the reset ---
transients
echo --- repro: a reset mid-rest ---
reset
transients
lockstep on

; --- and the rest of the wrecking --------------------------------------------
arena open 9 9
spawn skel_warrior 14 11
spawn skel_warrior 15 12
effect bleed 0 6 5
effect bleed 1 6 5
setstat 2 vitality 20
setskill 3 fire 9
setskill 0 conditioning 12
setsupply all food 20
setsupply all water 15
give waterskin 1
learn 3 fire
learn 3 project
autoattack on
freeze on
tally reset
forward 3
step 8
cast 3 fire project
step 2
; ...and what the GAME has running, for the reset to end (C294, C295, C297): an
; undo step, a throw's wait and a rest - under lockstep ON this time, the
; script's own mode, which baseline B must then read exactly as A does. 12,12
; is the open arena's centre.
setpool 0 health 10
editor place floors floor_slabs 12 12
editor place floors floor_temple 12 12
mappage close
throw rock
rest on
echo --- wrecked ---
party
supplies
monsters
tally
echo --- repro: wrecked, before the reset ---
transients

; --- and put it back ----------------------------------------------------------
reset
seed 7
echo === BASELINE B (after reset) ===
state
messages 10
party
supplies
regen
monsters
char 0
; THE MAP, and it is here because leaving it out is how the first version of
; this test passed on a world that was still an empty carved box. `arena` walls
; every cell and strips the map's fixtures, stairs and decorations; none of the
; probes above would show any of that. `mapinfo` prints the counts, and a
; walkability sample proves the CELLS came back and not just the records.
mapinfo
transients
; NOTHING after this line. The two baselines are compared line for line, so a
; trailing readout here would make block B one longer than block A and fail the
; check for a reason that has nothing to do with the reset.
